  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Arrival Cities shows a clear early impact profile: it has been downloaded 4,119 times, appears in public knowledge infrastructure through extensive Wikipedia-linked attention, and has already been cited once in the scholarly record. The book is also openly accessible in multiple instances, which likely supports its reach across research and teaching communities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Arrival Cities is a 2020 scholarly book from Leuven University Press that addresses exile, migration, modernism, and urban space, with particular relevance to geography, art history, and the study of cities. Its strongest signals are of use and visibility rather than mature citation accumulation: the download record from OAPEN indicates sustained readership, while the book\u2019s presence in public knowledge bases, especially Wikipedia-linked attention, suggests broader discoverability beyond strictly academic channels.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still small, with one OpenAlex-indexed citation so far. That citation appears in a 2023 book chapter on urban exile, indicating that the book is beginning to be taken up in adjacent scholarship on migration and transnational identity. At this stage, the record is best read as an early scholarly signal rather than evidence of settled canonical status.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Open access availability is another important part of the book\u2019s reach. The record shows multiple open instances, including access through the publisher DOI and a university repository copy, which helps explain the level of downloading observed. This kind of access often expands the audience for edited volumes and supports reuse in research, teaching, and public-facing scholarship.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding metadata also points to institutional support and research positioning: funding is associated with KU Leuven and the European Research Council, and library subject data places the book within themes of art and cities, immigration, social aspects, arts and society, and modernism. Taken together, the evidence shows a book with meaningful public visibility, active use, and an emerging citation footprint, with its influence likely still developing.</p>",
